#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193530} :activity/id #uuid "15829972-de8f-5398-aedc-ee127d53c955" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048304 :topic/id #uuid "92d07a33-6647-5dd0-9be4-64be21e6041d" :topic/math-academy-id 3950} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193530 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193827 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll learn how to use Riemann sums to approximate definite integrals of *composite* functions. As we'll see, this has some interesting applications.\n\nFor example, suppose $f(x)$ is a positive, continuous function. Some values for $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [1,5]$ are given by the following table:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nLet's use this data to approximate the following integral:\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^{5} x^2 \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nTo approximate this definite integral, we first define a new function $g(x)$ as\n\n$$\ng(x) = x^2\\cdot f(x)\n$$\n\nNow, our task is to approximate\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^{5} g(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nLet's approximate this integral using a left Riemann sum with $4$ equal subintervals.\n\nThe (regular) step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{5-1}{4} = 1\n$$\n\nWe can approximate this integral using a left Riemann sum with $4$ equal subintervals and step size $\\Delta x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^{5} g(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x \\approx (g(1) + g(2) + g(3) + g(4)) \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nLet's add a row to our table with values of $g(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| $g(x) = x^2 \\cdot f(x)$ | $3$ | $8$ | $9$ | $16$ | $50$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\int_{1}^{5}g(x)dx \\\\\n&\\approx (3 + 8 + 9 + 16) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 36 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 36\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now look at an example involving a right Riemann sum." :tutorial/id #uuid "c2502b79-d3d8-53e4-be62-b01752a16be9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6553 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1997ebaf-5bad-5fd7-875b-27650eb3f5b6" :step/math-academy-id 39566 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193531}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193531 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192700 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192408 :question/id #uuid "d8b6c55c-c272-5818-a113-40e6218fe12c" :question/math-academy-id "e-10484"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "824c497f-a01d-53c5-bcfe-e6cd35e69a88" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049386}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193067}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Riemann Sum of a Composite Function"} :step/id #uuid "99cb3798-47dd-5790-b2f7-fdfbcde6185f" :step/math-academy-id 39567 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193694}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193532 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192701 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192409 :question/id #uuid "c32c93a7-189d-5dc5-94a4-943ac5bbf29a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10485"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34555306-3c72-55ef-a59f-ab8d97ae4919" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503677} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511579}] :knowledge-point/title "Approximating Arc Length"} :step/id #uuid "7001e0a2-bc0e-5f22-adfb-6688e2d5c0c7" :step/math-academy-id 39568 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193547}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193533 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192702 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192410 :question/id #uuid "dbd945a6-786d-515e-8ecd-351dcf6b29f2" :question/math-academy-id "e-10486"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9946876-7435-589a-a18d-c89c3d790754" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050438}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193028} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511539} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511563}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Riemann Sum Gives an Overestimate or an Underestimate"} :step/id #uuid "d04db767-8034-5d9f-8871-0fb87a4da642" :step/math-academy-id 39569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193546 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192712 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192420 :question/id #uuid "54c6bc65-515f-52dd-8c37-ef6742464361" :question/math-academy-id "e-10520"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea7448d8-a235-52f4-a475-006943f8a142" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193068} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510425} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511455} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511578}] :knowledge-point/title "Approximating Volumes of Revolution"} :step/id #uuid "b9769694-1018-5682-8dd1-facf2627a6f4" :step/math-academy-id 39706 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193695}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193547 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193831 :tutorial/content "We've seen how to use Riemann sums to approximate areas under curves of composite functions. Our final task is determining whether these approximations overestimate or underestimate the true areas.\n\nWe start by considering *right* Riemann sums. Recall the following facts:\n\n- If a function is strictly *increasing*, the right Riemann sum *overestimates* the area under the curve $y = g(x)$.\n- If a function is strictly *decreasing*, the right Riemann sum *underestimates* the area under the curve $y = g(x)$.\n\nLet $f(x)$ be positive and strictly increasing for $x \\in [1,2]$, and differentiable for $x \\in (1,2)$. Now, consider the function\n\n$$\ng(x) = x^2 \\cdot f(x)\n$$\n\nSuppose we use a right Riemann sum to approximate the area under the curve $y = g(x)$ over $x\\in [1,2]$. Does the right Riemann sum overestimate or underestimate the true area?\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, we have $f'(x) \\gt 0$.\n- Differentiating our expression for $g(x)$ using the product rule, we get\n$g^{′}(x)|= (x^{2})^{′} \\cdot f(x) + x^{2} \\cdot f^{′}(x); = 2x \\cdot f(x) + x^{2} \\cdot f^{′}(x)$.\nWe now examine the sign of each factor in this expression to determine the sign of $g'(x)$.Since $f(x)$ is positive and strictly increasing over the interval $(1,2)$, we have\n$2x \\gt 0, \\qquad f(x) \\gt 0, \\qquad x^2 \\gt 0, \\qquad f'(x) \\gt 0$.\nHence, we obtain\n$g'(x) = \\underbrace{2x}_{\\gt 0} \\cdot \\underbrace{f(x)}_{\\gt 0} + \\underbrace{x^2}_{> 0} \\cdot \\underbrace{f'(x)}_{\\gt 0} = \\underbrace{2x \\cdot f(x)}_{\\gt 0} + \\underbrace{x^2 \\cdot f'(x)}_{\\gt 0} \\gt 0$.\n\nTherefore, since $g'(x) \\gt 0$, the function $g(x)$ is strictly increasing. Therefore, the right Riemann sum *overestimates* the area under the curve $y = g(x)$.\n\nLet's now look at an example involving a left Riemann sum." :tutorial/id #uuid "4017a527-6912-5d2d-a92d-3424c1399d67" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6582 :tutorial/title "Determining Overestimates and Underestimates"} :step/id #uuid "6cc57411-9166-578d-ad5d-18fd5906fee2" :step/math-academy-id 39707 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193533}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193694 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193904 :tutorial/content "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve $y=f(x)$, the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=a$ and $x=b$, as shown in the picture below.\n\n![](images/59/59f9a4f76889bf4323c7bcf7dad2dd5269698d94f294458eafdd4c5da0fa0580.png)\n\nWhen we rotate this region $360^\\circ$ about the $x$-axis (called the **axis of revolution**), we get a **solid of revolution,** as shown below.\n\n![](images/fc/fc2858aac81addf1d5c19cd3836361d42de4ce844117ab4d42960e45a274026d.png)\n\nIt can be shown that the volume $V$ of this solid of revolution is given by the formula\n\n$$\nV = \\pi \\int_{a}^{b} y^2 \\:\\textrm{d}x = \\pi \\int_{a}^{b} \\left[ f(x) \\right]^2 \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "169b0392-4eed-5202-921f-c379f4264c6d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7613 :tutorial/title "Volumes of Revolution"} :step/id #uuid "5ed00cb2-4d94-5a81-8e1c-488592b9d60c" :step/math-academy-id 44209 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193546}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193695 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193905 :tutorial/content "Consider the curve $y=f(x)$ between two points where $x=a$ and $x=b$.\n\n![](images/e7/e7fb3f6e11e0e2b51fc4ecae9827a4e378a7981450775f2b9330057c319688bf.png)\n\nThe length of the curve between these two points is known as the **arc length** of the curve.\n\nIt can be shown that the arc length is given by the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int_a^b \\sqrt{1+\\left[f'(x)\\right]^2}\\,\\mathrm{d} x\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x)$ is the derivative of $f(x)$.\n\nWe can use Riemann sums to approximate arc lengths. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "c6c4aa50-1ce8-52f8-aa97-b2bce6c85623" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7614 :tutorial/title "Arc Length"} :step/id #uuid "4da87fab-f669-5510-93d3-55fea59cbf65" :step/math-academy-id 44210 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193532}}] :activity/title "Approximating Areas Under Graphs of Composite Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192870}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510514} :activity/id #uuid "b6c8f1ee-bd3b-5b8f-a3ec-29aa9a0902db" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048304 :topic/id #uuid "92d07a33-6647-5dd0-9be4-64be21e6041d" :topic/math-academy-id 3950} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510490 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510383 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47c3c1ea-fcc8-5769-88c3-88f5edad13d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "26\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509833} {:answer/id #uuid "7b63efd9-e678-5ae5-857b-96e377f6cb45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509952} {:answer/id #uuid "89ea1e4b-2079-5ebf-8dbe-75e0c65f0d9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "29\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509988} {:answer/id #uuid "a41399a1-ef8a-537a-8f60-936cf9a829ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510029} {:answer/id #uuid "dbc2a1ce-cc77-5a90-ac10-c6984f36162e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510155}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509833} :answer-field/id #uuid "33c03e2d-ce2c-5b64-ac1b-19f84d3a3eea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3dca0c4b-ab3e-5cab-b892-e8bf877045ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-149431" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of a positive, continuous function $f(x)$ over the closed interval $[1,11].$ \nUsing a left Riemann sum with $5$ equal subintervals, estimate the volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by the curve $y=f(x),$ the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=1$ and $x=11$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ | $9$ | $11$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $2$ |\n\n*Hint: The volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by a curve $y=f(x),$ the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=a$ and $x=b$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis is given by*\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}[f(x)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, the volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by a curve $y=f(x),$ the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=1$ and $x=11$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{11}[f(x)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $g(x)={(f(x))}^{2}.$ The (regular) step size $Δx$ is given by \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{11-1}{5}=2.\n$$\n\nWe can approximate the integral above using a *left* Riemann sum with $5$ equal subintervals and step size $Δx$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{11}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x≈\\pi \\cdot (g(1)+g(3)+g(5)+g(7)+g(9))\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nWe set up a table with values of $g(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ | $9$ | $11$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $2$ |\n| $g(x)={(f(x))}^{2}$ | $1$ | $2$ | $0$ | $1$ | $9$ | $4$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our volume:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{11}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ≈\\pi \\cdot (g(1)+g(3)+g(5)+g(7)+g(9))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot (1+2+0+1+9)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot 13\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =26\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "32d2ec1a-d6ed-5482-879e-744367b94dca" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510553}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510511 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510395 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "190b0318-472c-530a-9953-285c1d80e520" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509737} {:answer/id #uuid "9ff2cf17-07cf-5a9d-b5df-c3c58e89ffd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510023} {:answer/id #uuid "cd101352-2412-5c2e-9e18-8982229905ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510119} {:answer/id #uuid "d0bd2120-8fb6-53d0-90aa-f72e30d237ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510131} {:answer/id #uuid "e99d7e0b-5dd6-505c-adc8-246fea845a95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510191}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510023} :answer-field/id #uuid "bda027a4-fbea-5ab2-9b7c-09170115f2b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "597bb006-761e-5b6e-a002-be65431d8129" :question/math-academy-id "q-149505" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be negative and strictly increasing for $x\\in [0,1],$ and differentiable for $x\\in (0,1).$ Suppose we approximate the area under the curve $y=g(x)=({x}^{2}-2)\\cdot f(x)$ over the interval $[0,1]$ using a right Riemann sum. Given that $g(x)>0,$ which of the following statements must be true?\n\nI. ${f}^{′}(x)>0$\n\nII. ${g}^{′}(x)>0$\n\nIII. The Riemann sum underestimates the true area." :question/worked-solution "We're given that $g(x)=({x}^{2}-2)\\cdot f(x)>0.$ Now, recall the following:\n\n- If $g(x)$ is strictly increasing, the right Riemann sum gives an *overestimate* of the area under the curve $y=g(x).$\n\n- If $g(x)$ is strictly decreasing, the right Riemann sum gives an *underestimate* of the area under the curve $y=g(x).$\n\nLet's now consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Differentiating $g(x)=({x}^{2}-2)\\cdot f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2}-2)^{′}\\cdot f(x)+({x}^{2}-2)\\cdot {f}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =2x\\cdot f(x)+({x}^{2}-2)\\cdot {f}^{′}(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x)$ is negative and strictly increasing over the interval $(0,1),$ we have \n\n$$\n2x>0,\\,f(x)<0,\\,{x}^{2}-2<0,\\,{f}^{′}(x)>0.\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)={{2x}_{⏟}}_{>0}\\cdot {{f(x)}_{⏟}}_{<0}+{{({x}^{2}-2)}_{⏟}}_{<0}\\cdot {{{f}^{′}(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}={{2x\\cdot f(x)}_{⏟}}_{<0}+\\,{{{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}^{′}(x)}_{⏟}}_{<0}<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${g}^{′}(x)<0,$ the function $g(x)$ is strictly decreasing. Therefore, the right Riemann sum gives an *underestimate* of the area under the curve $y=({x}^{2}-2)\\cdot f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "694eb003-d786-5734-a959-cc83c41aa69e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510514 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193027 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87f7157d-9ee6-5b05-9ef8-654c1987e877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190049} {:answer/id #uuid "b36c179e-6374-5bb0-8b12-b5bd9f9953b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190050} {:answer/id #uuid "d278bfce-4eb5-553f-8225-33457823e6d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190051} {:answer/id #uuid "1309590e-1bde-5aa8-8965-b5de6255f2c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the statements must be true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190052} {:answer/id #uuid "7bfc40b4-1eb7-5831-886c-c91fce39b4d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190053}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186190053} :answer-field/id #uuid "277d264b-fac7-5d04-a619-a104e80d1d5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "fbf952c0-e985-5c40-a219-a70a8e942eb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-148766" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be positive and strictly increasing for $x\\in [1,4],$ and differentiable for $x\\in (1,4).$ Suppose we approximate the area under the curve $y=g(x)=x\\cdot f(x)$ over the interval $[1,4]$ using a left Riemann sum. Given that $g(x)>0,$ which of the following statements must be true?\n\nI. ${f}^{′}(x)>0$\n\nII. ${g}^{′}(x)>0$\n\nIII. The Riemann sum underestimates the true area." :question/worked-solution "We're given that $g(x)=x\\cdot f(x)>0.$ Now, recall the following:\n\n- If $g(x)$ is strictly increasing, the left Riemann sum gives an *underestimate* of the area under the curve $y=g(x).$\n\n- If $g(x)$ is strictly decreasing, the left Riemann sum gives an *overestimate* of the area under the curve $y=g(x).$\n\nLet's now consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Differentiating $g(x)=x\\cdot f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =(x)^{′}\\cdot f(x)+x\\cdot {f}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =f(x)+x\\cdot {f}^{′}(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x)$ is positive and strictly increasing over the interval $(1,4),$ we have \n\n$$\nx>0,\\,f(x)>0,\\,{f}^{′}(x)>0.\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)={{f(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}+{{x}_{⏟}}_{>0}\\cdot {{{f}^{′}(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}={{f(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}+{{x\\cdot {f}^{′}(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${g}^{′}(x)>0,$ the function $g(x)$ is strictly increasing. Therefore, the left Riemann sum gives an *underestimate* of the area under the curve $y=x\\cdot f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} :step/id #uuid "6c9c828f-44ee-5495-86d5-5bda67c8798d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510539}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510539 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510425 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14818b9f-c464-5b94-bee6-e801c16f016a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "48\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509725} {:answer/id #uuid "4b983bb9-6a3a-5849-8009-b5191583e3f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "64\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509843} {:answer/id #uuid "55d254e1-55ef-55c0-a7d5-3dadc286794f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509865} {:answer/id #uuid "657b83be-229f-5a37-a103-a6cbf918ce96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509898} {:answer/id #uuid "fa4c20a8-cc6f-5131-826d-a35ace3db5e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510224}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509725} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a53dba6-93aa-5232-bd89-2d7c6d8b683b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "95ecf8d2-2252-5516-85e1-ebd3d6544d2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-149419" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of a positive, continuous function $f(x)$ over the closed interval $[0,15].$ \nUsing a right Riemann sum with $5$ equal subintervals, estimate the volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by the curve $y=f(x),$ the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=0$ and $x=15$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ | $12$ | $15$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $2$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $0$ | $2\\sqrt{2}$ | $1$ |\n\n*Hint: The volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by a curve $y=f(x),$ the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=a$ and $x=b$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis is given by*\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}[f(x)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, the volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by a curve $y=f(x),$ the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=0$ and $x=15$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{15}[f(x)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $g(x)={(f(x))}^{2}.$ The (regular) step size $Δx$ is given by \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{15-0}{5}=3.\n$$\n\nWe can approximate the integral above using a *right* Riemann sum with $5$ equal subintervals and step size $Δx$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{15}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x≈\\pi \\cdot (g(3)+g(6)+g(9)+g(12)+g(15))\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nWe set up a table with values of $g(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ | $12$ | $15$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $2$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $0$ | $2\\sqrt{2}$ | $1$ |\n| $g(x)={(f(x))}^{2}$ | $9$ | $4$ | $3$ | $0$ | $8$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our volume:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{15}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ≈\\pi \\cdot (g(3)+g(6)+g(9)+g(12)+g(15))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot (4+3+0+8+1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot 16\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =48\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c0e73eb7-4739-5f4b-84a0-6fccb98ec129" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510490}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510553 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510422 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f63c264-1a0f-552a-b8ad-9620bc2188c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509718} {:answer/id #uuid "13fe11c3-e0ca-520e-8103-0b01e9b72896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509722} {:answer/id #uuid "2455d6e0-88be-53fd-885e-23c520269d1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509767} {:answer/id #uuid "6581ba71-13b3-56cf-9b15-c01f14e38113" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509899} {:answer/id #uuid "b03ae649-783b-5d86-bc42-cc709124145e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510066}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510066} :answer-field/id #uuid "58e46613-c481-5ec5-9c32-4604b2d18598" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8b473789-e487-5a61-80e6-ac184764ee91" :question/math-academy-id "q-149458" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of a positive, continuous function $f(x)$ and its derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ over the closed interval $[0,9].$\nUsing a left Riemann sum with $3$ equal subintervals, estimate the arc length of the curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=0$ and $x=9.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $7$ | $10$ |\n| ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $2\\sqrt{2}$ | $0$ | $4\\sqrt{3}$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ |\n\n*Hint: The arc length of a smooth curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ is given by*\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, we have that the arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=0$ and $x=9$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{0}^{9}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $g(x)=\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}.$ The (regular) step size $Δx$ is given by \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{9-0}{3}=3.\n$$\n\nWe can approximate the integral above using a *left* Riemann sum with $3$ equal subintervals and step size $Δx$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{9}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x≈(g(0)+g(3)+g(6)\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nNow, we set up a table with values of $g(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $2\\sqrt{2}$ | $0$ | $4\\sqrt{3}$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ |\n| $g(x)=\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}$ | $3$ | $1$ | $7$ | $2$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our arc length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{9}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ≈(g(0)+g(3)+g(6))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(3+1+7)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =11\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =33\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e5bc175d-e11b-55ff-aaea-33f654704c94" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510511}}] :activity/title "Approximating Areas Under Graphs of Composite Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509689}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511628} :activity/id #uuid "c82e0f22-6e6f-5ddc-90a0-55c3a1ddea8c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048304 :topic/id #uuid "92d07a33-6647-5dd0-9be4-64be21e6041d" :topic/math-academy-id 3950} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511588 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511539 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d0f9bd4-1c42-518e-8a85-6fd510f818c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510874} {:answer/id #uuid "2e7b5dd1-45ce-5207-ba2f-a7fe58afd9ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510880} {:answer/id #uuid "a6cd4858-b628-5fda-bb17-ebfb005faec1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511134} {:answer/id #uuid "ce91986b-ea40-5ee9-8cff-03eb4608ba97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511210} {:answer/id #uuid "fa544d09-b82a-5a0e-a5ce-9b72cfaee527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511296}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510874} :answer-field/id #uuid "4633a0d6-26c7-541d-b8e0-d9639f7db195" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511348}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a8228bcb-8e29-55e3-8d2e-2b38cb08cc3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-149498" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be positive and strictly increasing for $x\\in [5,10],$ and differentiable for $x\\in (5,10).$ Suppose we approximate the area under the curve $y=g(x)={x}^{3}\\cdot f(x)$ over the interval $[5,10]$ using a right Riemann sum. Given that $g(x)>0,$ which of the following statements must be true?\n\nI. ${f}^{′}(x)<0$\n\nII. ${g}^{′}(x)<0$\n\nIII. The Riemann sum overestimates the true area." :question/worked-solution "We're given that $g(x)={x}^{3}\\cdot f(x)>0.$ Now, recall the following:\n\n- If $g(x)$ is strictly increasing, the right Riemann sum gives an *overestimate* of the area under the curve $y=g(x).$\n\n- If $g(x)$ is strictly decreasing, the right Riemann sum gives an *underestimate* of the area under the curve $y=g(x).$\n\nLet's now consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Differentiating $g(x)={x}^{3}\\cdot f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{3})^{′}\\cdot f(x)+{x}^{3}\\cdot {f}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)+{x}^{3}\\cdot {f}^{′}(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x)$ is positive and strictly increasing over the interval $(5,10),$ we have \n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}>0,\\,f(x)>0,\\,{x}^{3}>0,\\,{f}^{′}(x)>0.\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)={{3{x}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{>0}\\cdot {{f(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}+{{{x}^{3}}_{⏟}}_{>0}\\cdot {{{f}^{′}(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}={{3{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}+{{{x}^{3}\\cdot {f}^{′}(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${g}^{′}(x)>0,$ the function $g(x)$ is strictly increasing. Therefore, the right Riemann sum gives an *overestimate* of the area under the curve $y={x}^{3}\\cdot f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "0ed82c2f-3101-544e-b938-9685c699089a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511642}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511618 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511579 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c930864-9f25-5293-a719-9a80599eef5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510804}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510804} :answer-field/id #uuid "b068ad65-9909-5808-87be-751b4ea3f48f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511409}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f43ac4b9-2815-5dee-ad9d-013b2abdd370" :question/math-academy-id "q-346317" :question/problem "The arc length of a smooth curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThe table below gives the values of a positive, continuous function $f(x)$ and its derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ over the closed interval $[-4,2].$\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{7}{2}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $4$ |\n| ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $\\sqrt{63}$ | $\\sqrt{15}$ | $\\sqrt{8}$ | $\\sqrt{48}$ |\n\nUsing a left Riemann sum with $3$ equal subintervals, estimate the arc length $L$ of the curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=-4$ and $x=2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, we have that the arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=-4$ and $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{-4}^{2}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $g(x)=\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}.$ The (regular) step size $Δx$ is given by \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-(-4)}{3}=2.\n$$\n\nWe can approximate the integral above using a *left* Riemann sum with $3$ equal subintervals and step size $Δx$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{2}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x≈(g(-4)+g(-2)+g(0))\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nNow, we set up a table with values of $g(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $\\sqrt{63}$ | $\\sqrt{15}$ | $\\sqrt{8}$ | $\\sqrt{48}$ |\n| $g(x)=\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}$ | $8$ | $4$ | $3$ | $7$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our arc length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{-4}^{2}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ≈(g(-4)+g(-2)+g(0))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(8+4+3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =15\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =30\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "48ceae03-b320-5028-a92a-40ef95f39f8d"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511628 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511578 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29e0ebeb-fefa-5de4-b8b4-c14ae210cc54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "96\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510870} {:answer/id #uuid "504c6583-6b60-5cc7-a4f8-49752ce0621b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "76\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510954} {:answer/id #uuid "8bfc6107-bba4-5df6-bb3d-40aae12b2fd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "48\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511073} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a5fb43-9733-54d4-ae70-87014652f121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "104\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511217} {:answer/id #uuid "f127f49a-4ab2-5bca-8efb-9ad5241da47a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "52\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511277}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511217} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e36c481-1a7d-539b-a5da-9e256e0e030d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f302fed8-a5b6-5ab4-978d-2dce7c91e287" :question/math-academy-id "q-149437" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of a positive, continuous function $f(x)$ over the closed interval $[0,12].$ \nUsing a right Riemann sum with $3$ equal subintervals, estimate the volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by the curve $y=f(x),$ the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=0$ and $x=12$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $4$ | $8$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $3\\sqrt{2}$ | $0$ | $2\\sqrt{2}$ |\n\n*Hint: The volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by a curve $y=f(x),$ the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=a$ and $x=b$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis is given by*\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}[f(x)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, the volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by a curve $y=f(x),$ the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=0$ and $x=12$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{12}[f(x)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $g(x)={(f(x))}^{2}.$ The (regular) step size $Δx$ is given by \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{12-0}{3}=4.\n$$\n\nWe can approximate the integral above using a *right* Riemann sum with $3$ equal subintervals and step size $Δx$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{12}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x≈\\pi \\cdot (g(4)+g(8)+g(12))\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nWe set up a table with values of $g(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $4$ | $8$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $3\\sqrt{2}$ | $0$ | $2\\sqrt{2}$ |\n| $g(x)={(f(x))}^{2}$ | $1$ | $18$ | $0$ | $8$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our volume:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{12}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ≈\\pi \\cdot (g(4)+g(8)+g(12))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot (18+0+8)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot 26\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =104\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "715fe870-9c23-5686-9db6-2f539d5d9cb7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511588}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511642 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511563 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28b4554c-46dc-510f-ac58-1873cdfd838c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510865} {:answer/id #uuid "3a3204e2-55cf-5d83-b42c-28d5493becc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510915} {:answer/id #uuid "6f44229b-81f7-51d2-a925-37e447f0f037" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511013} {:answer/id #uuid "8475dc1b-d081-5b20-ad32-695e8ecb76fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511054} {:answer/id #uuid "fa9f2869-4ec7-5467-98e1-21e24df7e12a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511297}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511297} :answer-field/id #uuid "afc9afa2-0355-55d7-ab22-0b155eda768a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511408}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dc84fa2c-48fe-510a-8646-e9ae7a753f1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-149499" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be positive and strictly increasing for $x\\in [2,6],$ and differentiable for $x\\in (2,6).$ Suppose we approximate the area under the curve $y=g(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)$ over the interval $[2,6]$ using a right Riemann sum.  Given that $g(x)>0,$ which of the following statements must be true?\n\nI. ${f}^{′}(x)>0$\n\nII. ${g}^{′}(x)>0$\n\nIII. The Riemann sum underestimates the true area." :question/worked-solution "We're given that $g(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)>0.$ Now, recall the following:\n\n- If $g(x)$ is strictly increasing, the right Riemann sum gives an *overestimate* of the area under the curve $y=g(x).$\n\n- If $g(x)$ is strictly decreasing, the right Riemann sum gives an *underestimate* of the area under the curve $y=g(x).$\n\nLet's now consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Differentiating $g(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2})^{′}\\cdot f(x)+{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =2x\\cdot f(x)+{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}^{′}(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x)$ is positive and strictly increasing over the interval $(2,6),$ we have \n\n$$\n2x>0,\\,f(x)>0,\\,{x}^{2}>0,\\,{f}^{′}(x)>0.\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)={{2x}_{⏟}}_{>0}\\cdot {{f(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}+{{{x}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{>0}\\cdot {{{f}^{′}(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}={{2x\\cdot f(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}+{{{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}^{′}(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${g}^{′}(x)>0,$ the function $g(x)$ is strictly increasing. Therefore, the right Riemann sum gives an *overestimate* of the area under the curve $y={x}^{2}\\cdot f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "9982e8c2-d630-5fcb-a2a6-f49134260361" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511646}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511646 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511455 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e1dfa21-2b09-596a-bddf-9846939710c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "39\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510980}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510980} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a18cebd-84e4-5a3a-96a0-241925ce8033" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511335}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0272db1e-3587-51d4-ba80-cbc263d01f1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-346315" :question/problem "The volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by a curve $y=f(x),$ the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=a$ and $x=b$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}[f(x)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThe table below gives the values of a positive, continuous function $f(x)$ over the closed interval $[5,20].$ \n\n| $x$ | $5$ | $8$ | $11$ | $14$ | $17$ | $20$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n\nUsing a left Riemann sum with $5$ equal subintervals, estimate the volume $V$ of the solid generated when the region bounded by the curve $y=f(x),$ the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=5$ and $x=20$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, the volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by a curve $y=f(x),$ the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=5$ and $x=20$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {\\int }_{5}^{20}[f(x)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $g(x)={(f(x))}^{2}.$ The (regular) step size $Δx$ is given by \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{20-5}{5}=3.\n$$\n\nWe can approximate the integral above using a *left* Riemann sum with $5$ equal subintervals and step size $Δx$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pi {\\int }_{5}^{20}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x≈\\pi \\cdot (g(5)+g(8)+g(11)+g(14)+g(17))\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nWe set up a table with values of $g(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $5$ | $8$ | $11$ | $14$ | $17$ | $20$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| $g(x)={(f(x))}^{2}$ | $1$ | $4$ | $1$ | $3$ | $4$ | $9$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our volume:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{5}^{20}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ≈\\pi \\cdot (g(5)+g(8)+g(11)+g(14)+g(17))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot (1+4+1+3+4)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot 13\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =39\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a2816dcc-c5bf-5d5d-91a7-508f9fb5354d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511618}}] :activity/title "Approximating Areas Under Graphs of Composite Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510771}]}
